  "dom_id": "step-q116554",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
